[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In this video, I\'m going to help make your life easier flying the A1. There\'s a lot to remember before you start flying. So, I\'m going to break it down and make it really easy for you. I will also share with you how to get the best quality footage out of this drone. So, I\'ll share with you my best video and'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'photo settings. And if you want to make immersive 360 videos for the VR headsets, then I\'ll also share with you how to put together your anti-gravity footage in Premiere Pro using the special anti-gravity plug-in. This video'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is sponsored by Antiggravity. So, with that out of the way, let\'s get started. To set the best video settings, I will hold down the menu button on the remote to go to the quick menu and select'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera parameter settings. Here I am always using standard video mode. I set the resolution and frame rate to 8K 30 frames pers to get the most detailed video.'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'When filming on a bright sunny day, I like to use pro exposure mode because this will allow me to control the ISO and shutter speed. I like to set the ISO to 100 because this will give me the'

[01:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cleanest video possible without noise or grain. Then I will set the shutter speed to auto because when you fly through an environment,'

[01:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the exposure is always changing. it\'s getting brighter or darker. So, there\'s no one setting that works for the entire flight. So, I will let the drone adjust the exposure as it flies through darker'

[01:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and brighter areas. But because the drone tends to overexpose the sky, I set the EV from zero to minus0.7. And this will make the video a little'

[01:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bit darker so that the sky area does not blow out. This will make the shadow area much darker. But you can lift the shadows in editing to bring back that'

[02:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detail. For white balance, I like to set it to 5,000 Kelvin. And then from there, I can edit it in post.'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then go to settings, go to bit rate, and make sure this is set to high. This will allow more information to be'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stored about your video, which will give you a higher quality video overall. And those are all the best video settings. To set the best photo settings, I will'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hold down the menu button on the remote, go to camera parameter settings, and here there are three photo options. There is standard photo mode, which will'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take a single exposure photo at the exposure you set. There is HDR photo mode which will take a set of three photos and automatically merge them'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together into a high dynamic range photo. Or you can take a auto exposure bracketed photo which will also take three photos but you have to manually'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'merge them together in your favorite photo editing software. So, on a bright sunny day, I would recommend going for HDR photo mode, which will merge together the photos for you. Set the'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resolution to 55 megapixels to get the most amount of detail in your photo. Here, it\'s okay to use the auto mode. Set the EV to minus0.7'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so you don\'t blow out the highlights. You can set the white balance to auto or fix it at 5,000 Kelvin. And in the settings, go to photo storage format and'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make sure inspected to get the best quality photo possible. And those are the best photo settings. One of the most asked questions I get is'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what export settings do you use when you upload your video onto social media? So in this example, I have a 9x6 video which I want to upload onto Instagram stories. So I will go to export,'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'select custom export settings, select video quality. I will leave the resolution at 1080p. I personally set the bit rate to 25.'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Export this video to my phone and upload it to Instagram stories and it looks fine. Now some people will say no, you need to set a lower bit rate because then Instagram is not going to'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recompress the video and it\'s going to look better. So, in this case, you can set the bit rate to 10, export the video to your phone, and upload it to Instagram stories. So, you will need to test which one looks better, bit rate 10'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or bit rate 25. Now, let\'s say you want to export this shot for your long form YouTube video, and you\'re going to drop this shot into a third party video editor. So, I\'m going to choose the 16x9'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aspect ratio. Go to export. Select the custom export settings. Select video quality. I will choose the highest resolution 4K.'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I\'m going to choose the highest bit rate 150. Bit rate is basically a container. And by selecting the highest bit rate, I am selecting the largest container. If I'

[05:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'try and put my video in a small container like bit rate 10, then my large video can\'t fit in a small container. So then I need to throw away information about this video. And that\'s'

[05:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where pixelation comes from. So to make sure I can store the most information about my video, I\'m going to select the largest container and export this video to my phone. When you are in flight,'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re going to be concentrating and focusing on the screen inside the goggles. So, it\'s very important to make sure that the screen inside the goggles is comfortable for your eyes. So, make'

[06:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sure before you start flying, you use these twisty knobs to move the lenses left and right to adjust it for your pupil distance. And you can turn the twisty knobs left and right, and this'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will adjust the focus. So, make sure you nail this before flying for a comfortable experience and to make sure you don\'t get any ice strain when you are flying for a long period of time.'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There are a lot of steps to remember before you start flying. And this is my normal routine. So, first I will check that there is a micro SD card inside the drone and that it has sufficient'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'storage. Then I\'ll check the propellers to make sure that they are not damaged, broken, or bent. Then I will wipe the lenses because when you make the drone take off, when it'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lands and you put it in storage and take it out, then it might have smudges, fingerprints or dust. And this will affect the video quality. And then I will just tap on the battery'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to check the battery level. And now the drone is prepared. First I would turn on the goggles because this takes the longest to turn on. Then I turn on the controller. And then I turn on the drone'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'last because I\'m trying to preserve as much battery life as possible. Once they are all on and they connect, I am waiting for the signals on the screen to turn white. When the signal turns white,'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it means there is a strong connection between the RC and the A1 and there is enough GPS satellites to establish a strong connection in the sky. Once I'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have that signal, I will then use the controller to take off. I then wait for the home point to be updated. You don\'t need to fear about losing the A1 because as long as the home point is set before'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you start flying, the A1 already knows where to come back to if it loses signal. But there\'s one really important setting you need to choose before you start flying. So let\'s say in your'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment, the tallest thing in your environment is 40 m. And let\'s say when you\'re flying with the drone, it\'s at 30 mters and then it disconnects. Then it needs to fly back to the home point. The'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'return to home altitude needs to be set higher than the highest thing in your environment. So if the highest thing in your environment is 40 m, set the return to home altitude to 50 m. So when the'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drone loses connection at 30 m, it will then rise to the return to home altitude at 50 m, fly over all the obstacles, and then land wherever it has to land. To'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get the best range between the goggles and the A1, you need to make sure these antennas are up and that you are always facing the drone.'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you face 90° away from the drone, then the signal is going to get weaker. If you face 180° away from the drone, then the signal is the weakest. So, you'

[09:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'always want to make sure that you are facing towards the drone and that there is nothing in between the goggles and the drone. That means no walls, no trees, no buildings, etc. When you take'

[09:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'off and land with the A1, you want to make sure you\'re doing it on flat ground where there are no hazards, especially like rocks or dust. If you take off on dusty terrain, then your lenses are'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to get dirty before you even start recording. So, the solution to this problem is to use a launch pad like this. So, you put this on the ground and take off from this instead. And this'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will prevent the dust from going onto the lens. Now, this is the anti-gravity launchpad. And if you haven\'t got your A1 already, you can get this launchpad free with your order using my affiliate'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'link in the video description. Always keep an eye on the battery level. The A1 will tell you when it thinks it\'s time to come back, but you do need to assess the situation for yourself. So, let\'s say that going from A to B took 15% of'

[10:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the battery. I will assume that I need 30% battery to go from B to A. The reason for this is that maybe going from'

[10:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A to B, the wind helped push the drone to get there. Therefore, it was using less power. Therefore, to come back from B to A, the drone is going to use more power to fight against the wind. So, by'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'having double the amount of battery, I can give myself confidence that I can bring the drone back safely. Now, in a situation where the drone is struggling to come back against the wind, you\'ll'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need to put the remote into sport mode. This is going to give the A1 more power and speed to fight through the wind and come back to the home point. There are three speed modes on the A1. There is'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cinematic mode, normal mode, and sport mode. I suggest when learning to stick to normal mode. This will give you a nice and controlled flight. You don\'t need to hold the trigger down to go full'

[11:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speed. You can hold it down halfway and this will give you a nice and controlled speed. I suggest using cinematic mode when you want to make immersive 360 videos for the VR headsets for a nice'

[11:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slow and smooth flight without any jerky movements. And on the opposite end, you have sport mode, which will unlock the highest speed on the drone. Flying as high as you can is not going to get you'

[11:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the best shots. It\'s actually quite the opposite because this is a 360 camera. So, when you\'re flying really high, there\'s really nothing to see. You have to actually fly close to things to see'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the detail. But flying close to things will depend on the drone regulations in your area. To get the best stitching results, then you want to make sure that the main focal point of your video is'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'either facing the top lens or the bottom lens. If you\'re trying to film your main subject in between the lenses, then you need to make sure there is at least a 2 m gap to reduce the amount of stitching'

[12:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'artifacts. Because it is a 360 drone, you don\'t need to worry about framing while you are flying. You can fly first and reframe later. That means while you are flying, you have the freedom to turn'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your head to look around and see what\'s around. And this is good for two reasons. You can look around for obstacles. So you can fly off to the sides, or you can look around for new shots. To be honest, it\'s the most'

[13:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simple flight path that make for the most cinematic shots. There\'s no need to do acrobatics in the air. So to get the most cinematic shots, all I do is fly in a straight line forward.'

[13:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I will rise and I will fall and I will orbit to go around something. And just by doing that, you can get some'

[13:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really cool, amazing shots. And the rest is done in editing. For beginners who have never flown a drone before, there is now a flight simulator within the goggles, so you can practice flying the'

[13:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A1 in a virtual space before you fly the A1 in the real world. To access the flight simulator, I will tap the menu button and go to flight simulator.'

[13:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Wait for it to load. Here I can see the A1 in front of me. And just like taking off the real A1, I'

[14:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will push up twice on the controller to start the motors and then push up to make the drone hover. I will use the controller to point in the direction I want to fly in and pull'

[14:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the trigger to fly in that direction. Now, let\'s say, for example, that I want to fly in reverse. To do this, I\'m going to point the'

[14:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controller behind me 180°. I will look back at the clock tower, pull the trigger, and now it looks like I am doing a reverse shot.'

[14:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, feel free to practice in this virtual environment as much as you want because it\'s not real. So, now let me give you a quick tutorial on how you can turn your anti-gravity footage into an'

[14:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'immersive 360 video using the anti-gravity Premiier Pro plug-in. To edit your anti-gravity videos in Premiere Pro, you\'ll need to download a plug-in. So, to do this, go to the'

[15:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anti-gravity website, the download section, scroll down, and download and install the anti-gravity Adobe Premier Reframe plug-in. Once you have installed'

[15:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the plug-in, open up Premiere Pro. Go to new project. Name your project. Choose a folder to save your project to and click create.'

[15:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Tap skip. Go to your folder with your anti-gravity footage. Drag the footage to import it directly into Premiere Pro. Double click the footage.'

[15:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Here I will make a 10-second selection and drag the video into the timeline. And now I have a project with the'

[16:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'correct settings automatically at 8K 30 frames pers. If your computer is slow or lagging, then you can turn on proxies'

[16:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you can change the playback resolution to 1/8. To add text into your 360 video, tap the text tool,'

[16:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'draw a box, type the text. To change the text size, I will highlight the text, go to effect controls,'

[16:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go to the text dropdown, and I\'ll change the font size to 170. Then I will adjust the text box to fit around the text.'

[16:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Center the text. Go to graphics and titles. Align to video frame. center horizontally and also center vertically.'

[17:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then to make sure the text appears properly in 360 video, I will go to effect controls. Here I will type in VR plane and drag'

[17:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the VR plane to sphere effect onto the text. Change the scale to 100°. And now when you preview the 360 video,'

[17:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the text will appear correctly. To make the preview window wider, right click,'

[17:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go to VR video settings, change the horizontal to 160 and the vertical to 90. And now you can repeat this process for'

[18:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all your 360 videos. To color grade anti-gravity footage, I like to use the best 360 L, which I will link in the video description. So to add the L, go to the creative tab, click'

[18:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look, go to browse, select the L, click open, and now you have a color grade. You can adjust the'

[18:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'strength by changing the intensity to a lower number. To export your 8K 360 video for YouTube, select the timeline, go to export,'

[18:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'choose a file name, choose where to save the video to. Choose the format H.265. In basic video settings, click match'

[18:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'source. Click more. And here, uncheck profile. Uncheck level.'

[19:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Select the level 6.2. Scroll down. Select the target bit rate 200.'

[19:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'check video is VR and export your video. Then you\'ll be able to upload this video onto YouTube and it will display as a 360 video. If'

[19:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to reframe your 360 video, then first make a new sequence. And if you\'re making this for Tik Tok or Instagram stories, then change the frame'

[19:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rate to 29.97. Change the frame size to 1080 by 1 1920 and you can rename your sequence name'

[20:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and click okay. Then I will drag the video into the timeline.'

[20:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Keep existing settings and then I will zoom into the timeline. Go to effects, type anti-gravity.'

[20:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Click and drag the anti-gravity refframe onto the video. Then go to effect controls. Reset the scale to 100.'

[20:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Scroll down. Change the resolution to 1080p. 9x6 aspect ratio, which is the same as our timeline.'

[20:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then here you can play with the settings whether you want no distortion or distortion and the field of view you prefer. And here you have the controls'

[21:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to pan, tilt, move up and down and roll left or right. So if you want to add a pan movement,'

[21:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can use the key frames here and then move forward in the timeline. Change the pan.'

[21:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So now when I play this back, there is a left to right pan. That\'s all my tips and tricks. I hope you found this video useful.'

[21:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Anti-gravity is currently having a sale with 15% off the A1. And if you use my affiliate link in the video description, you can also get a launchpad for free. So, if you\'re interested, check out my'

[21:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'affiliate link in the video description and I\'ll see you in the next video.'
